<h3>Open Closures</h3>
<p>A more internal new thing is the &#8220;open closure&#8221; concept, courtesy of Stefan Monnier.  It&#8217;s like a struct that you can funcall?  Or like a function object that you can interrogate about various data and metadata.</p>
<p>So &#8212; a closure that&#8217;s open, so it has the best name ever.</p>
<h3>Variable Pitch Tables</h3>
<p>Mixing fonts, images and text size in tabular displays has always been a pain in Emacs, so I&#8217;ve now written a new package for that: vtable.  The thing I&#8217;ve been using to test it is the ewp package, which I&#8217;m writing this very blog post from:</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/2022-04-10.png"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/2022-04-10.png" alt="" width="840" height="326" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-90104" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/2022-04-10.png 1749w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/2022-04-10-300x116.png 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/2022-04-10-1024x398.png 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/2022-04-10-768x298.png 768w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/2022-04-10-1536x596.png 1536w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>All nice and lined up.</p>
<p>Emacs has had tabulated-list-mode for quite a while, but in addition to not dealing with proportional fonts, it&#8217;s also just awkward interface-wise: It expects to own the entire buffer it lives in, so you can&#8217;t have a table in the middle of some other text, and it stores all the data in buffer-local variables, so you can&#8217;t have several tables in the same buffer.  And interacting with the table always seems to happen via setting some obscure variable and then calling some other obscure function, so I wanted to make an interface that fixes all of that, in addition to making it trivial to create simple tables, and easy to create complex tables.</p>
<p>My inspiration for the interface design was originally the Common Lisp/LispWorks table library developed for my former employer by Espen Vestre, but it diverged quite a bit in the end.  The idea is basically still the same: You have data, you have the formatted data, and then you have the data that&#8217;s being presented, and you can alter these things at several levels.  (Which is important when resizing a column and you also want to resize images shown in a column, for instance.)</p>
<p>Here&#8217;s the simplest table possible, I guess:</p>
<pre>(make-vtable
 :objects '(("Foo" 1034)
            ("Gazonk" 45)))</pre>
<p>This displays as:</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/2022-04-10-1.png"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/2022-04-10-1.png" alt="" width="283" height="129" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-90105" /></a></p>
<p>You&#8217;d want a header, though, so:</p>
<pre>(make-vtable
 :columns '("Name" "ID")
 :objects '(("Foo" 1034)
            ("Gazonk" 45)))
</pre>
<p>Which is:</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/2022-04-10-2.png"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/2022-04-10-2.png" alt="" width="287" height="135" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-90106" /></a></p>
<p>And here&#8217;s how simple a very simple version of <tt>C-x C-b</tt> could be implemented:</p>
<pre>(make-vtable
 :columns '("Name" "Size" "File")
 :objects (buffer-list)
 :actions '("k" kill-buffer
            "RET" display-buffer)
 :getter (lambda (object column vtable)
           (pcase (vtable-column vtable column)
             ("Name" (buffer-name object))
             ("Size" (buffer-size object))
             ("File" (or (buffer-file-name object) "")))))</pre>
<p>Which gives us:</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/2022-04-10-3.png"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/2022-04-10-3.png" alt="" width="840" height="577" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-90107" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/2022-04-10-3.png 1502w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/2022-04-10-3-300x206.png 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/2022-04-10-3-1024x704.png 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/2022-04-10-3-768x528.png 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>Finally, here&#8217;s a very simple image browser which displays most of the significant features:</p>
<pre>(make-vtable
 :columns `(( :name "Thumb" :width "500px"
              :displayer
              ,(lambda (value max-width table)
                 (propertize "*" 'display
                             (create-image value nil nil
                                           :max-width max-width))))
            (:name "Size" :width 10
                   :formatter file-size-human-readable)
            (:name "Time" :width 10 :primary ascend)
            "Name")
 :objects-function (lambda ()
                     (directory-files "/tmp/" t "\\.jpg\\'"))
 :actions '("RET" find-file)
 :getter (lambda (object column table)
           (pcase (vtable-column table column)
             ("Name" (file-name-nondirectory object))
             ("Thumb" object)
             ("Size" (file-attribute-size (file-attributes object)))
             ("Time" (format-time-string
                      "%F" (file-attribute-modification-time
                            (file-attributes object))))))
 :separator-width 5
 :keymap (define-keymap
           "q" #'kill-buffer))</pre>
<p>This results in (if you&#8217;ve been watching Japanese 60s movies):</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/2022-04-10-4.png"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/2022-04-10-4.png" alt="" width="840" height="518" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-90109" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/2022-04-10-4.png 1726w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/2022-04-10-4-300x185.png 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/2022-04-10-4-1024x632.png 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/2022-04-10-4-768x474.png 768w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/2022-04-10-4-1536x949.png 1536w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/2022-04-10-4-825x510.png 825w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>And increasing the size of the first column allows the displayer to resize the thumbnails:</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/2022-04-10-5.png"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/2022-04-10-5.png" alt="" width="840" height="751" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-90110" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/2022-04-10-5.png 1858w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/2022-04-10-5-300x268.png 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/2022-04-10-5-1024x916.png 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/2022-04-10-5-768x687.png 768w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/2022-04-10-5-1536x1374.png 1536w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>And so on.</p>
<h3>Make Commands Query</h3>
<p>You can now make any command query you before it&#8217;s executed.  For instance, if you want to be queried before doing <tt>C-x C-c</tt>, you can just say</p>
<pre>(command-query 'save-buffers-kill-terminal)</pre>
<p>in your init file.</p>
<h3>More Electric Completion</h3>
<p><video autoplay loop muted controls><source src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/completion.mp4" type="video/mp4"></video></p>
<p><tt>M-&lt;down&gt;</tt> and <tt>M-&lt;up&gt;</tt> can now be used to navigate completions directly, which makes for quicker selection between candidates.  This, and many other user interface improvements in completion is courtesy of Juri Linkov and Jimmy Aguilar Mena.</p>
<h3>Clean CI</h3>
<p>After fixing some obscure bugs (only appearing in a few configurations) and a couple of not-so-obscure bugs (along with some judicious disabling of some tests that were timing out on EMBA), we now have a green board:</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/2022-03-13.png"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/2022-03-13.png" alt="" width="840" height="499" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-89744" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/2022-03-13.png 2509w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/2022-03-13-300x179.png 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/2022-03-13-1024x609.png 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/2022-03-13-768x457.png 768w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/2022-03-13-1536x914.png 1536w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/2022-03-13-2048x1219.png 2048w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>Whoho!  That means that we hopefully can start to send out nagging mails to people that commit something that makes things go red.</p>
